---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import snapshot from "../_data/snapshots/live-7d.json";
import type { Snapshot } from "../types";

const data = snapshot as Snapshot;
const topThree = data.leaderboard.slice(0, 3);
---
<Base title="Home">
  <div class="banner">
    <strong>Preview build.</strong> This site is built against dummy snapshot data while the
    upstream API ships. Numbers do not reflect real contributor activity yet.
  </div>

  <h2>A handful of labs can see how AI agents actually behave</h2>
  <p>
    Every day, AI agents do real work. They call tools, take wrong turns, fail in
    specific ways, and sometimes get things right. All of that leaves a record of
    what actually happened — and almost all of it lands in the logs of the companies
    that built the agents.
  </p>
  <p>
    That gap is not a detail. Whoever can watch how agents fail is the only party
    equipped to make them work better, audit them honestly, or build anything that
    competes. Access to this evidence is the bottleneck on everything downstream of
    it: safety research, independent evaluation, open models, informed regulation.
    Trace Commons exists to open it.
  </p>

  <h2>What Trace Commons is</h2>
  <p>
    A register of AI agent work, held under terms its contributors set. Contributors
    run <a href="https://github.com/iqlusioninc/ironclaw">Ironclaw</a> locally to
    capture and scrub the records their own agents produce. Only the scrubbed
    envelope leaves the machine, carrying a <strong>consent scope</strong> that
    decides what any future query is permitted to do with it. The scope travels with
    the record and can be withdrawn.
  </p>
  <p>
    Two gates decide what gets filed: one asks whether the record is genuinely
    different from everything already in the register, the other whether it is
    substantive work rather than template-shaped filler. Both must pass. The gates
    are not quality control on suppliers — they are what keeps the register worth
    opening. A corpus of near-duplicates teaches no one anything.
  </p>
  <p>
    A <strong>Trace Credit</strong> is the signed, on-chain record that one of a
    contributor's submissions was accepted. Credits are non-transferable. They are
    the contributor's standing claim on what the register earns.
  </p>

  <h2>Where we actually are</h2>
  <p>
    A register has to exist before it can be opened. Right now a small invited cohort
    is building it, which means the most important thing on this page — open access
    to the evidence — is not yet something you can exercise. We would rather state
    the commitments below now, before they are convenient, than describe them once
    they cost us nothing.
  </p>

  <h2>What we commit to</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>
      <strong>Contributors set the terms of use, and those terms bind.</strong> A
      record's <a href="/about/data-policy">consent scope</a> decides what any query
      may do with it — debugging, evaluation, ranker training, training-data
      inclusion. Nothing exceeds the scope it was given. Scopes are revocable, and
      withdrawal takes effect at the next snapshot. This one is enforced in the
      pipeline today, not promised.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Corpus-level analytics stay open to everyone.</strong> What the register
      contains and how it is growing stays readable without an invite, an account, or
      a payment. That is the point of the thing; putting it behind a gate would
      reproduce the asymmetry we are trying to close.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>When buyers pay to query the register, that flows to contributors.</strong>
      Credits are the mechanism. Trace Commons is not a platform collecting rent on
      other people's work.
    </li>
  </ol>

  <h2>What is open right now</h2>
  <p>
    Two surfaces, already public, no invite required: <a href="/analytics">corpus
    analytics</a> showing what the register holds in aggregate, and the
    <a href="/leaderboard">leaderboard</a> of contributors who chose to be visible.
    Both are built from snapshots that apply the deployment's release guards, so
    aggregates never resolve to individual traces.
  </p>

  <h2>Top contributors (last 7d)</h2>
  {topThree.length === 0 ? (
    <p class="muted">No public contributors yet.</p>
  ) : (
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>#</th>
          <th>Handle</th>
          <th>Score</th>
          <th>Accepted</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {topThree.map((entry) => (
          <tr>
            <td>{entry.rank}</td>
            <td><a href={`/contributors/${entry.display_handle}`}>{entry.display_handle}</a></td>
            <td>{entry.score.toFixed(1)}</td>
            <td>{entry.accepted_count}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  )}
  <p><a href="/leaderboard">See the full leaderboard →</a></p>

  <h2>How to join</h2>
  <p>
    The pilot is invite-only while the register is being built. If you have an invite code and a workload token from
    the pilot operator, the contributor-side setup is documented in
    <a href="https://github.com/TraceCommons/trace-commons-server/blob/main/docs/operator/pilot-contributor-onboarding.md">pilot-contributor-onboarding.md</a>.
    Contributing is private by default. Once a few of your submissions have cleared
    both gates, you can choose to be publicly visible — or stay unlisted and
    contribute anyway.
  </p>
</Base>
